column400:
03/Feb/19 13:08;ivera;I have done some changes on the approach. The first attempt was not using the docId for tie-breaking when the dimension that we were partitioning were equal. So I added that to the approach and it made me clear the need to wrap the byte arrays into {{ByesRef}} objects.

That gives me the possibility to buffer the reads from the {{OfflinePointReader}} to reduce the number of reads from disk. This proves to give a very good improvement in performance. A further improvement is that this buffer is shared by all readers (there is only one reader at a time).

Finally, I have updated the formula that calculates the size of the buffers. Currently it is defined as:

 
{code:java}
maxPointsSortInHeap = (int) (0.5 * (maxMBSortInHeap * 1024 * 1024) / (bytesPerDoc * numDataDims));
{code}
 

 0.5 because we hold a copy of the data in every recursion. And {{numDataDims}} because we have a copy for each dimensions. In this new approach, we don't hold a copy of each dimension so we can remove it but we need to multiply by a further 0.5 because the radix partitioning can hold up to two buffers. Therefore the new formula hold as:

 
{code:java}
maxPointsSortInHeap = (int) (0.25 * (maxMBSortInHeap * 1024 * 1024) / (bytesPerDoc));
{code}
 

With these changes, the Lucene geo benchmarks looks like:


||Approach||Index time (sec): Dev||Index Time (sec): Base||Index Time: Diff||Force merge time (sec): Dev||Force Merge time (sec): Base||Force Merge Time: Diff||Index size (GB): Dev||Index size (GB): Base||Index Size: Diff||Reader heap (MB): Dev||Reader heap (MB): Base||Reader heap: Diff||
|points|216.6s|226.8s|-5%|119.8s|155.2s|-23%|0.55|0.55|0%|1.57|1.57|0%|
|shapes|318.2s|639.2s|-50%|208.8s|587.0s|-64%|1.29|1.29|0%|1.61|1.61|0%|
|geo3d|240.7s|364.8s|-34%|134.1s|274.2s|-51%|0.75|0.75|0%|1.58|1.58|0%|


It shows a really nice speed up in all dimensions. The performance now it is more or less linear to the size of the point (numDims * bytesPerDim + Integer.BYTES) which is what we would expect for this approach.

