column399:
06/Feb/19 12:49;alapin;[~vozerov]
{quote} we need to perform some minor cleanup, remove exception throwing from the hot path, remove intermediate conversions from the hot path{quote}
Fixed. 
{quote}and clarify several complex conversions{quote}
* _Investigate other vendors: what happens for out-of-bound case? e.g. convert(257) -> error or -1 or something else?_
postgresql, mssql and H2 - exception,
{code}
postgres=# select cast (9223372036854775807 as int);
ERROR:  integer out of range
{code}
mysql, mariadb and oracle - cut to max;
{code}
MariaDB [(none)]> select cast(18446744073709551619 AS unsigned);
+----------------------------------------+
| cast(18446744073709551619 AS unsigned) |
+----------------------------------------+
|                   18446744073709551615 |
+----------------------------------------+
1 row in set, 1 warning (0.000 sec)
{code}

* _Remove and user UUID.fromString(). If there are any subtle differences between UUID.fromString and H2 logic, they should be explained in comments so that we understand what part of conversions are absent in our logic._
Given method is more fault tolerant than more common <code>UUID.fromString()</code>.For example it supportsString represendation of UUID-without-hyphens conversion, that is not supported by mentioned above <code>UUID.fromString()</code>.

* _Investigate other vendors: what happens for -1?_
Some databases doesn't support Boolean datatype:
https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/5.5/en/cast-functions.html#function_cast
https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/5.5/en/cast-functions.html#function_convert
https://mariadb.com/kb/en/library/convert/
https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/data-types/data-types-transact-sql?view=sql-server-2017
https://docs.oracle.com/cd/B28359_01/server.111/b28318/datatype.htm#CNCPT1828
PostgreSQL uses same logic as we do: postgres 0 -> f, [-1, 1, ...] -> t Same is about H2.

