 Review comment:
   > is there good documentation on when to use which?

   The best right now is this:
   https://devblogs.microsoft.com/dotnet/understanding-the-whys-whats-and-whens-of-valuetask/

   In short:
   - Start with `Task and `Task<T>`.  They provide the best usability.  If you don't care about getting the best performance, aren't on a hot path, etc., you're done.
   - If the method in question returns a T value and has a high likelihood of completing synchronously, strongly consider using `ValueTask<T>`, in order to avoid the `Task<T>` allocation per `T`.  There are a few exceptions to this, namely cases where you or the runtime can cache every possible or likely T to be returned, e.g. if T == bool.
   - If the method in question is willing to sign up for increased complexity and has an effective/efficient means for pooling objects, consider using `ValueTask`/`ValueTask<T>`; the implementation can return a `ValueTask`/`ValueTask<T>` that wraps an `IValueTaskSource`/`IValueTaskSource<T>` which it reuses over and over for many calls.
   - If the method in question is virtual or abstract, consider whether some implementer may feel strongly about one of the above cases.

   As an example, Stream.Read/WriteAsync now have overloads that return `ValueTask<int>` and `ValueTask`, respectively.  `MemoryStream`'s overrides of these always complete synchronously, so those overrides never need to allocate tasks.  `NetworkStream`'s overrides these and uses a pooled `IValueTaskSource` implementation to avoid allocation even for asynchronous completion, but at the cost of non-trivial implementation complexity.

